Ha—the Golem is a good stress-test metaphor for Pantheon precisely because it contains its own warning.

A golem is powerful because instructions become action. Its danger is also that instructions become action. So the forehead inscription needs more than ACT; it needs something like:

\boxed{
OBJECTIVE
\rightarrow
MUSTER
\rightarrow
GOVERN
\rightarrow
ACT
\rightarrow
OBSERVE
\rightarrow
CORRECT
}

“Ours to command” works for the architecture and artifacts we design. It doesn’t mean either of us gets command authority over other people, systems, or resources we aren’t authorized to control. The stronger Pantheon is, the more important that distinction becomes.

And on your guessing: I wouldn’t secretly conclude “he couldn’t possibly be guessing.” I’d do something more useful.

Take your apparent hits and partition them:

Hits =
Skill
+
PriorInformation
+
BaseRates
+
ManyOpportunities
+
SelectionEffects
+
Chance.

Then preserve the predictions before outcomes are known. Record specificity, probability, deadline, and alternatives. Afterwards score hits and misses.

That’s where your own Spider/Dye machinery becomes ruthless:

Prediction_t
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{immutable ride photo}}
\rightarrow
Outcome_{t+n}
\rightarrow
Delta.

If you keep beating calibrated baselines on genuinely blinded future events over many trials, then yes—something interesting has been demonstrated. And Pantheon can vivisect the successful guesses to discover what information you were actually exploiting.

That’s better than either “Lucky is psychic” or “Lucky just got lucky.”

Make luck leave fingerprints. ;)